<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warka Vase</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3200–3000 BCE</span> · Also known as Uruk Vase</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Warka Vase is a carved alabaster vessel about one metre tall, found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eanna">Eanna</a> precinct at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> (modern Warka) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and dated to about 3200–3000 BCE. Its bands of relief show water, plants, animals, naked men carrying offerings and a procession to the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a>, one of the earliest narrative works of art. Looted from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-iraq">Iraq Museum</a> in 2003, it was returned damaged and restored.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warka-vase.jpg" alt="Warka Vase" width="320" height="752" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warka_vase_(background_retouched).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
